Z||000019||Monkeyman||08-09-2000||11:21 AM||||Umm...Patrick's Dad?  I think we decided that the only GT bodystyle in '86 was a fastback.  I'm pretty sure we decided that the ".5" or "1/2" only referred to the fact that Pontiac switched manual trannys half way through the year (from the Isuzu to the Getrag).  Someone correct me if I'm wrong.  I really don't want to spend 1/2 the day searching through old threads to back myself up.||||reg||1
Z||000020||stimpy||08-09-2000||06:54 PM||||I'm with Monkey, don't know where it was established that there is such thing as a notchback 85 GT unless it's an 86 SE with some 85 GT panel windows on it. <BR>As far as the 86.5 thing goes, I disagree w/ you Dave. All early fastbacks are 86 1/2 GTs. This is because they were released too late to join the rest of Pontiacs 86 lineup. New car models are usuually available around September of the year before. It is common to call a model year a 1/2 year when it is released after the first of the year. Example- there is no 64 Mustang, the first Model of the Mustang is a 64 1/2. <BR>It is true Pontiac did change some specs on the late 86.5 GTs. That does not change the model year designation.<P>Of course there is always a distinct possibility that I am full of crap.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Fiero86||08-09-2000||08:25 PM||||anyone ever hear of a TR (turbo racing)Piranha kit that goes on Fieros? I wanna know if i have a decent kit. It says on the rear...Limited Production Number: 8.<P>||||reg||1
